// AFTER the POSIX validation above (a doomed rename never reaches here)
// and independently of the source gate (two independent scope-exits).
//
// Use the COALESCED gate (the same one an unlink uses), NOT the plain one:
// for a SHALLOW overwrite (the grant-root IS `dest_ino`'s parent) it
// rebuilds the grant-root's child list EXCLUDING `dest_ino` and rotates
// only the SURVIVING subtree — so the to-be-removed destination node is
// never re-keyed. The plain gate rotates `dest_ino`'s OWN node moments
// before it is unlinked; on macOS FUSE-T the SMB overwrite-rename then
// `open()`s that destination and reads its freshly-rotated IPNS record
// before it can resolve (AES-GCM/EIO), failing the whole rename. Linux's
// kernel `rename` never opens the destination, which is why only macOS
// consistently surfaced this. Leaving `dest_ino`'s read plane intact makes
// that `open` succeed. (A DEEP covered overwrite returns `Ok(false)` and
// falls back to the plain full-subtree rotation, unchanged.)
//
// The coalesced "skip the parent relink" signal is intentionally IGNORED:
// unlike a pure delete, an overwrite-rename still must republish the parent
// to carry the source node's rename, which the relink below performs under
// the rotation-refreshed key.
if let Some(dest_ino) = dest_ino {
    if crate::write_ops::grant_scope::run_scope_exit_gate_coalesced(fs, dest_ino, newparent)
        .is_err()
    {
        reply.error(libc::EIO);
        return;
    }
}

